WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.420
JENNY BROWN: Are you suffering
death by a million paper cuts?

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:06.180
Hopefully not because
that sounds awful.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.030
But if that's a metaphor
for your small file uploads

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.880
messing with your performance,
then you're in luck.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.740
Today on "Cloud
Storage Bytes," we're

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.620
diving deep into the world
of optimizing your small file

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:17.310
uploads.

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:18.420
So stick around.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:21.348
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:24.590
Let's say you're
a biotech company,

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.120
and your main
product is a software

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:29.810
that works with DNA across
multiple distributed machines.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:30.960
Everything is great.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.230
But whenever you add a
data set, your clients

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:36.830
are stuck waiting and
waiting to begin computation.

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:38.240
You've checked
your architecture,

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:39.860
and it's pretty straightforward.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.230
A new data set comes
in, gets divided,

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:45.080
and uploaded to Cloud Storage,
triggering a Pub/Sub alert

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:46.940
for your clients
to start computing.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.790
So what's the problem here?

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:50.360
And this is the tricky bit.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:53.720
The amount of work each client
is responsible for changes

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:57.180
depending on the number of
clients connected to do work.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:00.500
So having more clients
leads to smaller work blocks

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:03.170
as the clients balance
out that workload.

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:04.879
This means that
your software will

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:07.790
take ages to upload all
the files to Google Cloud

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:09.530
Storage in regions
with the highest

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:11.630
number of available cores.

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:13.640
So let's say you've
got a data set

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.910
with files of various sizes,
and you upload each file

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:21.172
to a regional bucket over
and over and over again.

00:01:21.172 --> 00:01:22.880
If you were to take
a look under the hood

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:24.680
while this upload
takes place, you'd

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:28.760
see that as file size increases,
so does the throughput speed,

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:32.090
meaning smaller files are going
to bog you down like a death

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:36.680
by a million paper cuts, which,
once again, sounds rough.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.290
The reason for smaller
files slowing things down

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:41.750
is because of the amount
of transactional overhead

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:43.550
for uploading those files.

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:46.640
The smaller each individual
file is the more times

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:50.860
this transactional overhead
adds up and gives you issues.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:52.580
There is good news
here, and that comes

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.710
by way of parallel uploads.

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:57.240
As we hinted at in
an earlier episode,

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:00.050
you can use the dash
M option in gsutil

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:03.350
to perform a parallel copy
and dramatically increase

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:05.630
the performance of your uploads.

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:07.970
If you were to revisit your
example of the data set

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:11.250
from earlier and take another
peak right under the hood

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.250
this time using
parallel uploads,

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:15.710
you'd see significantly
higher throughput,

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:17.720
as that pesky
transactional overhead

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:21.200
is mitigated across the
multiple parallel threads.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.960
Problem solved and
paper cuts avoided.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:27.260
But how does this help
with large file uploads?

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:30.510
We have answers, but that's
a totally different episode.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.630
If you want to know more
about parallel uploads

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:35.600
for small files, check out
the link in the description.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:37.653
And don't forget to
subscribe, give us a like,

00:02:37.653 --> 00:02:40.070
and tell us what more you want
to learn about Google Cloud

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:41.060
Storage.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:43.280
Thank you for joining us
for this quick byte of cloud

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:43.790
storage.

